From: FurryMileon <furrymileon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] entry: Add a direct-upcall mode to Syscall User Dispatch

Syscall User Dispatch delivers every blocked syscall as SIGSYS.  Wine
relies on it for Windows-style syscall stubs, and some Windows games
(seen with THE FINALS) issue them at 100-200k/s on their main thread.
Each trap costs a full signal round trip: signal frame setup with an
XSAVE of the complete extended state, the handler, and rt_sigreturn
with XRSTOR.  On Zen 4 with the TSA and SRSO mitigations that is about
1.5 us per trap even for an empty handler, and over a third of the game
thread's CPU time.

Add an opt-in mode that resumes userspace at a registered handler
instead:

  prctl(PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH_HANDLER, handler, flags, 0, 0)
        Registers the per-task handler (user address).  0 clears it;
        clearing while upcall mode is armed fails with -EBUSY.  The
        handler is not inherited: clone and exec reset it together with
        the rest of the dispatch state.

  prctl(PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH,
        PR_SYS_DISPATCH_UPCALL_{EXCLUSIVE,INCLUSIVE}_ON, off, len, sel)
        Same as the existing modes, but a blocked native 64-bit syscall
        resumes at the handler.  Fails with -EINVAL if no handler is
        registered or on non-x86_64 kernels.

With flags = 0 the handler is entered with the register state of the
SYSCALL instruction: RCX = address after the syscall, R11 = RFLAGS,
RAX = syscall number, everything else untouched.  RCX != RIP makes
do_syscall_64() leave through IRET.

With flags = PR_SYS_DISPATCH_HANDLER_PUSH_RET the handler is entered
like a CALL: the return address is pushed below the 128-byte red zone,
RCX = RIP = handler and R11 = RFLAGS, which is what SYSRET loads, so the
kernel leaves through SYSRET instead of IRET.  The return address is
ordinary stack memory above the new RSP, so signal frames and contexts
changed by a debugger keep it like any call frame.  If the stack cannot
be written the syscall is reported with SIGSYS, with nothing changed.
Kernels without the flag reject a non-zero flags argument, so userspace
can probe for it.

ia32 syscalls (including int $0x80 from 64-bit code) and syscalls
issued with TF set keep using SIGSYS: the handler could not tell the
ABI apart or recover the argument in RCX, a 64-bit handler cannot run
on a 32-bit code segment, and with TF the first handler instruction
would trap before it could save the single-step state the way a SIGSYS
handler does.

PTRACE_GET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH_CONFIG reports the upcall modes.  It
also reports inclusive ranges as the inclusive range they were armed
with: they are stored inverted, and the exclusive form it reported so
far is rejected by PTRACE_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH_CONFIG, so a
get/set round trip (checkpoint/restore) failed for them.

The handler option uses a magic number and the modes carry bit 31, so
neither can collide with values upstream assigns later; kernels without
this patch return -EINVAL for both.

Measured by the original author in a KVM guest on a Ryzen 9 7950X3D
(2M traps, empty handler): native getpid 224 ns, SIGSYS dispatch
1482 ns, upcall dispatch 244 ns.  tools/sudtrap.c measures all modes.

Based on CachyOS/linux#15 by FurryMileon (the flags = 0 mode is
unchanged, so CachyOS/wine-cachyos#37 works as is).  Changes for
linux-lunar: the PUSH_RET mode, the inclusive-range ptrace fix, a
handler flags argument, documentation of the handler's entry state
(DF, ENDBR64, shadow stack).

